# 1. Symmetric vs Asymmetric Encryption
=======================================================================================================================================
## What is Encryption?
Encryption transforms readable data (**plaintext**) into unreadable data (**ciphertext**) using a cryptographic algorithm and a key.

```text
Plaintext → Encryption → Ciphertext
Ciphertext → Decryption → Plaintext
```
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## A. Symmetric Encryption
Symmetric encryption uses **one shared key** for both encryption and decryption.
### Working
```
          Secret Key
              ↓
Plaintext → Encrypt → Ciphertext
              ↓
         Decrypt
              ↓
          Plaintext
```

### Popular Algorithms
| Algorithm | Key Size        | Status           |
| --------- | --------------- | ---------------- |
| DES       | 56-bit          | Obsolete         |
| 3DES      | 168-bit         | Legacy           |
| AES       | 128/192/256-bit | Recommended      |
| Blowfish  | 32-448 bit      | Older but secure |

### Advantages
✅ Very fast
✅ Efficient for large files
✅ Low CPU usage

### Disadvantages
❌ Secure key sharing is difficult
❌ If key is stolen, all data is compromised
### Real-World Uses
* VPN encryption
* Disk encryption
* Wi-Fi security (WPA2/WPA3)
* Database encryption
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## B. Asymmetric Encryption
Uses **two mathematically related keys**.

| Key         | Purpose |
| ----------- | ------- |
| Public Key  | Encrypt |
| Private Key | Decrypt |

### Working
```
Public Key
     ↓
Encrypt Message
     ↓
Ciphertext
     ↓
Private Key
     ↓
Decrypt Message
```

### Popular Algorithms
| Algorithm      | Purpose                 |
| -------------- | ----------------------- |
| RSA            | Encryption & Signatures |
| ECC            | Modern encryption       |
| Diffie-Hellman | Key Exchange            |
| ElGamal        | Encryption              |

### Advantages
✅ Solves key distribution problem
✅ Supports digital signatures
✅ Enables secure communication over insecure networks

### Disadvantages
❌ Slower than symmetric encryption
❌ More computationally expensive
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## Symmetric vs Asymmetric Comparison

| Feature            | Symmetric | Asymmetric |
| ------------------ | --------- | ---------- |
| Keys Used          | One       | Two        |
| Speed              | Fast      | Slow       |
| Key Distribution   | Difficult | Easy       |
| Digital Signatures | No        | Yes        |
| Data Encryption    | Excellent | Good       |
| Examples           | AES, DES  | RSA, ECC   |

